==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=GI}}
{{rfc|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
'''GI''' was originally an initialism for ''Galvanized Iron''; during the 1930s the usage expanded to stand for ''Government Issue''; by World War II it had become a nickname for the military personnel themselves.

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|G.I.}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{label|en|military}} {{initialism of|lang=en|[[general]] [[issue]]}} Given to a soldier by the army (''as'' an item), rather than brought from home; ''or'' used by the army (''as'' a procedure), and different from its [[civilian]] counterpart.
#: ''Soldiers counted cadence by chanting: “'''GI''' brush / And '''GI''' comb / Gee, I wish that I was home!”''
# {{label|en|medicine}} {{initialism of|lang=en|gastrointestinal}}
#: ''I was queasy after my upper '''GI''' series, but it far outshone the lower one.''
# {{label|en|US}} {{initialism of|lang=en|government issue}}
# {{label|en|US|military}} {{initialism of|lang=en|general infantry}}
#: '''''GI''' Joe.''

===Noun===
{{wikipedia|GI (military)}}
{{en-noun}}

# A member of the U.S. military.
# One who is [[demanding]] and [[strict]], in the manner of a stereotypical member of the military.
# {{initialism of|lang=en|[[galvanize|galvanized]] [[iron]]}}
# {{misspelling of|Gl|lang=en}}, the prefix for [[Gliese]] catalog of nearby stars

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{label|en|military}} To [[clean]] aggressively.
#: ''Top, get the unit to '''GI''' this AO before the old man arrives.''

